This is a defender. It protects the argument from an objection rather than linking the evidence to the conclusion. When an argument says something was *impossible*, look for the answer that rules out one more way it could have happened. And notice the ruling-out word, "nobody." Remove it and the objection appears.$pilot$), ($pilot$D$pilot$, $pilot$A common myth would *help* Mikkeli by explaining the similarities innocently. But his defense doesn't depend on it; his argument is about access. Negate (D), so there's no common myth, and his "I had no access" argument is exactly as strong as before.$pilot$), ($pilot$E$pilot$, $pilot$This is the most tempting wrong answer, because it sounds like another language door. But Old Icelandic is extinct and only related to an *earlier* form of Norwegian. Negate it, so Mikkeli knows Old Icelandic, and he still can't read modern Norwegian. Halden's book came out 20 years before his, not 800. Knowing an ancient cousin of the language doesn't let him read the book.$pilot$)) v(letter,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT112-S1-Q3$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_choice_note